GZIP(1)                        P32W Unix                         GZIP(1)

NAME
       gzip, gunzip, zcat - compress or expand files

SYNOPSIS
       gzip [-cdfknNqtv0..9] [file ...]
       gunzip [-cfknNqtv] [file ...]
       zcat [-fqtv] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       gzip compresses each file with the deflate method and writes
       it in gzip format (RFC 1952) as file.gz, exchangeable with the
       Mac's gzip. The original is removed. The new file gets the
       original's permission bits and modification time. The gzip
       header records the original's name (without its directory)
       and modification time.

       gunzip, which is gzip -d, expands each file.gz back to file,
       file.tgz to file.tar and file.z to file, and removes the
       compressed one. A file holding several gzip members one after
       another is expanded into their concatenation; anything after
       the last member is ignored, with a warning. The CRC and
       length of every member are checked. The expanded file gets
       the compressed one's permission bits and the modification
       time recorded in the header (if there is none, or with -n,
       the compressed file's).

       zcat, which is gzip -dc, expands the files to the standard
       output and leaves them in place.

       The program looks at the name it is called by (the last
       component of argv[0]); the three names are links to one
       program.

       With no file arguments, or for a file argument -, gzip
       compresses (or with -d expands) the standard input to the
       standard output. It will not write
       compressed data to a terminal unless -f is given.

       Only regular files are handled; others are reported and
       ignored. gzip will not compress a file whose name already ends
       in .gz, .tgz or .z, nor expand one whose name does not (except
       with -c or -t), and will not replace an existing output file
       unless -f is given.

OPTIONS
       Options may be combined (-dc); -- ends them.

       -c     Write to the standard output and keep the input files.
              The suffix checks are skipped, so zcat file and
              gzip -c file.gz both work.

       -d     Decompress (expand).

       -f     Force: replace an existing output file, and compress to
              a terminal.

       -k     Keep the input files.

       -n     No name: when compressing, record neither the name nor
              the time in the header; when expanding, give the file
              the compressed file's time, not the recorded one.

       -N     Name: when expanding, name the file by the name
              recorded in the header (in the compressed file's
              directory) instead of the compressed file's name less
              its suffix; gunzip -N x.gz may make notes.

       -q     Quiet: no warnings (a file ignored for its suffix or
              its type, trailing garbage). The exit status is the
              same; errors are still reported.

       -t     Test: expand each file, check its CRC and length, and
              throw the result away. Nothing is written or removed.

       -v     Verbose: for each file, on the standard error, the
              compression saved in percent (when compressing), then
              "replaced with" the new file if the input was removed,
              or "created" and the new file with -k; with -t, "OK".

       -0 .. -9
              Compression level, from fastest (-1) to best (-9);
              the default is -6. -0 stores the data without
              compressing it (still in gzip format).

EXAMPLES
       gzip notes                    notes -> notes.gz
       gzip -9k big.log              best compression, keep big.log
       gunzip src.tgz                src.tgz -> src.tar
       zcat man.gz | more            read without expanding on disk
       gzip -t *.gz                  check the files
       gunzip -N x.gz                x.gz -> its original name
       tar cf - src | gzip > src.tgz compress a pipe

EXIT STATUS
       0 if all went well (trailing garbage is not an error); 1 if
       a file could not be compressed or expanded (not in gzip
       format, a CRC or length error, damaged data, an existing
       output file, a wrong suffix, not a regular file; also with
       -q), or compressed output would go to a terminal; 2 for a
       bad option. A failed output file is removed.

SEE ALSO
       tar(1), zip(1), unzip(1), deflate(3), crc32(3)

BUGS
       Unlike GNU gunzip, which by default gives the expanded file
       the compressed file's time, gunzip uses the time recorded in
       the header (as GNU gunzip -N does); -n gives the GNU default.

       There is no -l (list), -r (recurse), -S (suffix), -L or -V,
       and no long --options. Owners are not kept. The .Z suffix of
       compress(1) and its LZW format are not understood.

       File names are limited to 255 characters.
